's a good chance none of the problems are solved, but given that multiple AI systems may contribute and AI capabilities improve, perhaps it's 50% by 2050. \n\nAnother angle: since there are six problems, the probability of any one being solved might be higher. For example, even if the chance for each is 5%, over 60 years, the chance could be higher. However, the timeline we're looking at is up to 2050, 25 years away from 2025. \n\nI think after all that, my final answer is:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.02,\n  \"2030\": 0.08,\n  \"2035\": 0.20,\n  \"2040\": 0.35,\n  \"2050\": 0.55\n}\n\nBut I need to make sure these numbers are non-decreasing. Let me verify:\n\n0.02 (2026) < 0.08 (2030) < 0.2 (2035) < 0.35 (2040) < 0.55 (2050). Yes, that's non-decreasing. \n\nAlternatively, maybe adjust the increments to be more reasonable. For example, 2026: 2%, 2030: 5%, 2035: 10%, 2040: 20%, 2050: 35%. That's another possible set. \n\nBut I'm not sure exactly. Given the uncertainty, I think the key is to have a functional progression over time. Let me check the example in the prompt. Suppose the user expects a gradual increase.  \nMaybe better to round the numbers and ensure each subsequent year has a higher probability. \n\nFinal answer after considering all factors:\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.02, \"2030\": 0.06, \"2035\": 0.15, \"2040\": 0.30, \"2050\": 0.50}",
